title crypto/secp256k1_ecdsa_sign.rye — ECDSA signing in pure Rye, byte-for-byte parity-GREEN
prompt take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the road ahead — Rye prioritized, Glow on green-witnessed Rye; send each finished increment; stop at custody gates
think Read the wave doc, itinerary, and CRYPTO.md front door — the secp256k1 tower stands base field · group law · scalar ladder · scalar field mod n · ECDSA verify, all GREEN. The Lindy-first crux-first next rung is ECDSA signing: it completes the signature scheme Bitcoin/Ethereum use, parallels ed25519_sign, and stands on already-GREEN HMAC-SHA-256 plus the scalar field.
think Studied Zig's std/crypto/ecdsa.zig deterministicScalar — it is NOT strict RFC 6979 but the "Deterministic ECDSA with Additional Randomness" DRBG: an extra 32-byte zero noise block sits between the domain byte and the key, and the raw SHA-256 digest feeds the DRBG rather than RFC 6979's bits2octets(h1 mod n). To claim the strongest parity — byte-identical signatures — I reproduced Zig's DRBG exactly rather than textbook 6979.
think Signing math: k from the DRBG, R = k·G over the GREEN ladder, r = affine x of R mod n, s = k⁻¹·(z + r·d) mod n, no low-s normalization (Zig does none). The scalar module exports reduce·mul·invert·is_canonical but no modular add, so z + r·d is a carry-add into a 64-byte little-endian buffer folded by sc.reduce.
think Custody check: signing touches a secret, yet exactly like ed25519_sign it signs only with a caller-supplied TEST key (Zig's deterministic key pairs in the selftest), never the maintainer's identity key — so this is not a custody gate. Signing a real identity stays gate #3/#4.
obs GREEN on metal: our authored Rye signature equals Zig's std.crypto.sign.ecdsa.EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256 signature byte-for-byte across eight deterministic key pairs × five messages, derive_public matches Zig's public key, the signature round-trips through our own GREEN verifier, signing is deterministic, a tampered message fails, a zero key is refused. Two compiler-free first-build passes after fixing a temporary-address (&sha.hash(...)) into a bound local.
obs Constant-time honesty: signing is the sharpest secp256k1 horizon — the k·G ladder and k⁻¹ inversion branch on the secret; correct and parity-GREEN yet NOT timing-safe. Recorded in CONSTANT_TIME.md beside ed25519_sign; the fe/scalarmul/scalar rows updated from "public today, signing a later round" to "a secret now flows, active timing horizon."
